(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-ophthalmic-pathophys-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Ophthalmic Pathophysiology Quiz 2 — Clin Path I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which lid deformity goes with dacryoadenitis?","choices":["An S-shaped lid deformity","An inward-turning lid margin","An outward-turning lid margin","A drooping upper lid from levator weakness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — dacryoadenitis is inflammation or infection of the lacrimal gland, causing superotemporal orbital rim pain and an S-shaped lid deformity. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Where do impulses travel after leaving the optic nerve?","choices":["To the frontal eye fields","To the superior colliculus alone","To the occipital cortex","To the temporal cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the signal runs from the optic nerve through the chiasm and optic tract to the lateral geniculate nucleus, then via the optic radiations to the occipital cortex. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which three structures make up the uvea?","choices":["The choroid, the retina and the sclera","The ciliary body, the lens and the zonules","The iris, the cornea and the conjunctiva","The choroid, the ciliary body and the iris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the choroid, ciliary body and iris form the uvea, the vascular middle tunic, with the highly vascularized choroid nourishing the retina. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which condition can cause amblyopia?","choices":["Raised intraocular pressure","Posterior vitreous detachment","Drusen beneath the retinal pigment epithelium","Uncorrected strabismus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — uncorrected strabismus is a cause of amblyopia, the loss of best-corrected acuity from abnormal visual processing in the critical pediatric period. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are the risk factors for rhegmatogenous detachment?","choices":["Chronic corticosteroid use, Down syndrome and ultraviolet light exposure","Posterior vitreous detachment, age, severe myopia, trauma and lattice degeneration","Diabetes, prior vitrectomy and retinal trauma surgery","Sarcoidosis, malignant hypertension and melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — posterior vitreous detachment, age, severe myopia, trauma and lattice degeneration set up the full-thickness break through which liquefied vitreous enters. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which glands are involved in an external as opposed to an internal hordeolum?","choices":["External involves a meibomian gland; internal involves the glands of Zeis or Moll","Both involve the lacrimal gland","Both involve the tarsal plate itself rather than any gland","External involves the glands of Zeis or Moll; internal involves a meibomian gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an external hordeolum is an acute abscess of the Zeis or Moll glands at the lash follicles, and an internal one involves a meibomian gland in the tarsal plate. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which three interneuron types modulate the retinal signal before it reaches the ganglion cells?","choices":["Bipolar, horizontal and amacrine cells","Bipolar, Müller and astrocyte cells","Horizontal, amacrine and Purkinje cells","Rod, cone and ganglion cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bipolar, horizontal and amacrine cells modulate the photoreceptor signal and route it to the ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which description fits a chalazion?","choices":["A cholesterol-filled plaque along the nasal eyelid","Redundant lid skin with prolapse of orbital fat","A chronic, non-tender, sterile granulomatous lipogranuloma of a blocked meibomian gland","An acute, tender, focal abscess of a gland sitting at the eyelid margin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — sterile and granulomatous is what separates it from an infection. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the symptom set of acute angle-closure glaucoma?","choices":["Flashing lights, a sudden shower of floaters, and a curtain falling across the field","Gradual central distortion with a scotoma","Severe eye pain, headache, halos around lights, cloudy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil, and nausea and vomiting","Painless gradual bilateral blurring with glare at night"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — iris bombé blocks aqueous outflow totally, and the rapid pressure spike above 50 mmHg produces pain, headache, halos, a cloudy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil and vomiting. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Diabetic retinopathy is the leading cause of new-onset blindness in which group?","choices":["Children under sixteen worldwide","Adults over fifty worldwide","United States adults aged twenty to seventy-four","United States adults aged over seventy-five years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — chronic hyperglycemia damages retinal capillaries, and diabetic retinopathy is the leading cause of new-onset blindness in United States adults aged twenty to seventy-four. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the natural course of a subconjunctival hemorrhage?","choices":["Recurrent by definition, so it requires long-term ophthalmology follow-up","Benign, resolving over one to two weeks with bruise-like color changes","Progressive, threatening vision within days","Permanent, leaving a fibrovascular scar"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — blood leaked from ruptured episcleral or conjunctival capillaries lies beneath the conjunctiva and clears in one to two weeks, changing color like a bruise. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What can a myopic patient see well?","choices":["Objects far away","Neither near nor far without correction","Only objects at the extreme periphery","Objects up close"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — myopia is nearsightedness, so near vision is the preserved one. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which virus is the typical cause of viral conjunctivitis?","choices":["Herpes simplex virus","Varicella zoster virus","Cytomegalovirus","Adenovirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — adenovirus is the viral cause of conjunctivitis, which can also be bacterial, allergic or toxic, and shows hyperemia without severe deep pain. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does aqueous humor nourish?","choices":["The optic nerve head","The extraocular muscles","The avascular lens and cornea","The retina and the choroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — made by the ciliary body, aqueous flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber and nourishes the lens and cornea, both of which lack blood vessels. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the functional consequence of presbyopia?","choices":["Inability to focus on near objects, so reading glasses or bifocals are needed","Inability to focus on distant objects, so distance vision blurs first","Loss of peripheral vision","Loss of color discrimination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the sclerotic lens and ciliary muscle can no longer accommodate, so near objects cannot be focused and readers or bifocals are needed. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which pairing of patient group and organism carries particular risk in keratitis?","choices":["Immunosuppressed patients, from Candida","Contact lens wearers, from Pseudomonas","Contact lens wearers, from Staphylococcus aureus","Elderly patients, from Streptococcus pneumoniae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — contact lens wearers carry a Pseudomonas risk, and keratitis is an emergency because it can progress to stromal ulceration, perforation and endophthalmitis. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What drives the change from non-proliferative to proliferative diabetic retinopathy?","choices":["Bacterial collagenase digestion of the retinal surface","Severe ischemia upregulates vascular endothelial growth factor, producing neovascularization","Rising intraocular pressure forces new vessels to form","Accumulation of lipofuscin and apolipoprotein beneath the pigment epithelium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — chronic hyperglycemia occludes capillaries, severe ischemia upregulates vascular endothelial growth factor, and fragile new vessels grow on the disc and retina. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What are drusen composed of?","choices":["Fibrin and platelet aggregates","Calcium and phosphate crystals","Cholesterol esters, as in xanthelasma","Lipofuscin and apolipoproteins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — drusen, the hallmark of dry macular degeneration, are discrete yellow extracellular deposits of lipofuscin and apolipoproteins beneath the pigment epithelium. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A temporal lobe lesion affecting part of the optic radiation produces which defect?","choices":["Monocular blindness","Contralateral superior quadrantanopsia, or pie in the sky","Contralateral inferior quadrantanopsia instead","Bitemporal hemianopsia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a temporal lobe lesion, surgery or middle cerebral artery stroke at the temporal optic radiation causes contralateral superior quadrantanopsia, pie in the sky. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is iris bombé?","choices":["Forward displacement of the iris against the cornea","Backward displacement of the iris against the lens","Atrophy of the iris stroma after an acute attack","Adhesion of the iris to the corneal endothelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — in angle closure, pupil dilation pushes the iris forward against the cornea, and this iris bombé blocks aqueous outflow totally so pressure spikes. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How does panophthalmitis differ from endophthalmitis?","choices":["It spares the sclera","It involves all layers of the globe and may extend into orbital tissue","It involves only the vitreous","It is limited to the anterior segment and spares the vitreous"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pan- means all: panophthalmitis involves every layer of the globe and can spread into the orbit, beyond the intraocular infection of endophthalmitis. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which description fits the rods of the retina?","choices":["About 120 million, for dim light and peripheral vision","About 6 million, for dim light and peripheral vision","About 120 million, for color vision and sharp acuity","About 20 million, for color vision in bright light"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — about 120 million rods give high sensitivity for dim light and night vision, and they are found in the peripheral retina. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"By what mechanism does diabetes mellitus cause cataract?","choices":["Retinal ischemia releases vascular endothelial growth factor into the lens","Excess glucose is converted to sorbitol, causing osmotic swelling of the lens","Excess glucose glycates the lens capsule until it ruptures","Hyperglycemia causes crystallin proteins to aggregate directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — in diabetes, excessive glucose is converted to sorbitol, and the resulting osmotic swelling of the lens produces cataract. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What causes astigmatism?","choices":["Irregular corneal or lens curvature creating non-spherical focal points","An axial globe that is too long","An axial globe that is too short","Loss of lens elasticity with age, so the lens can no longer change shape"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — irregular curvature of the cornea or lens creates non-spherical focal points instead of the single focal point needed for a sharp image. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which systemic diseases are strongly linked to scleritis?","choices":["Rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis and systemic vasculitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus, psoriatic arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis","Diabetes mellitus and hypertension","Sarcoidosis and choroidal melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — scleritis, an emergency of the deep sclera with boring pain and a risk of scleral melting, is strongly linked to rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis and systemic vasculitis. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What are the two pathophysiological causes of strabismus?","choices":["Raised intraocular pressure with progressive optic disc cupping and field loss","Lens opacification and loss of the red reflex","Extraocular muscle imbalance and cranial nerve palsy of the third, fourth or sixth nerves","Retinal detachment and vitreous hemorrhage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — strabismus is mechanical: an extraocular muscle imbalance or a palsy of the third, fourth or sixth cranial nerve misaligns the visual axes. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of the most common, senile form of cataract?","choices":["Chronic ultraviolet-driven oxidative damage alone","Progressive insoluble aggregation and deposition of lens crystallin proteins","Osmotic swelling of the lens driven by sorbitol accumulating inside it","Rupture of the lens capsule after blunt trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — senile cataract, the most common form, results from progressive insoluble aggregation and deposition of lens crystallin proteins, opacifying the lens. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What single feature separates a pterygium from a pinguecula?","choices":["The pterygium encroaches onto the cornea; the pinguecula does not","The pterygium is yellow; the pinguecula is triangular","The pterygium is painful; the pinguecula is painless","The pterygium is bilateral; the pinguecula is unilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both are fibrovascular proliferations from chronic ultraviolet, wind and dust exposure, but only the pterygium encroaches onto the cornea and can impair vision. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How does dry macular degeneration affect vision?","choices":["Rapid central vision loss over days","Loss of peripheral vision first, with the central field spared until late","Sudden painless monocular blindness","Slow gradual loss of central detail, with distortion and a scotoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — slow degeneration of photoreceptors, pigment epithelium and choroid causes gradual loss of central detail, with metamorphopsia and scotoma. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of presbyopia?","choices":["The cornea flattens progressively with age, reducing its refractive contribution","The retinal pigment epithelium thins with age","The lens loses elasticity and the ciliary muscle loses accommodation, through sclerosis","The axial globe shortens with age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — sclerosis stiffens the lens and the ciliary muscle loses accommodation, so the eye can no longer focus on near objects. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"}]};
